{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789307662,"nonce":"1mLfXQugEpYQj/0W8gqeh73J0j0=","title":"Emitted Haxe classes: inheritance, overloads, and a dynamic face","message":"The build macro (haxe/caribou/Bridge.hx) emits every class under\ncaribou.Ref and gives a Wren subclass its superclass's members by\ncopying them, because a Haxe constructor must call its parent's and a\nWren subclass's constructor is its own. So `Panel is Hud` in Wren is not\n`Panel extends Hud` in Haxe: a Panel is not assignable to a Hud. A way\nthrough: a protected constructor path that binds no object, so the\nsubclass's `super()` is a no-op and its own native binds the face.\n\nTwo Wren members of one name and different arities cannot both be one\nHaxe method; the second gets the arity appended (`draw1`). A static and\nan instance member of one name clash the same way.\n\nA foreign object of a class the program does not declare arrives as a\nplain caribou.Ref, which has no members. It needs the dynamic\noperations of the protocol as methods: call, get, set, and iteration.","files":null}]}